WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.239
OK, let's get into audience questions for
the week.

00:00:03.239 --> 00:00:08.790
Many of them related to the really tragedies
that we observed in the United States over

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:10.020
the last week and a half.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.400
First question, David.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.450
We have the biggest military and most militarized
police in the world.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.390
Why couldn't the Capitol Police keep rioters
out?

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:20.670
What the hell happened?

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:27.830
And this is a question that became an important
one very quickly after we as we were witnessing

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:32.349
the events of January 6th, the riotous insurrection
fomented by President Donald Trump.

00:00:32.349 --> 00:00:38.129
And it is true, the government spends hundreds
of billions on defense, on the military, Department

00:00:38.129 --> 00:00:43.449
of Defense, Pentagon to Department of Homeland
Security, on and on and on.

00:00:43.449 --> 00:00:49.179
How is it possible that you ended up with
a situation where a mob reaches the Capitol,

00:00:49.179 --> 00:00:56.420
the vice president and members of Congress
are are there Secret Service and other evacuations?

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.410
And and within minutes, this mob I mean, we
were watching it.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:05.160
And it wasn't very long after the debate started
into the objections over Arizona that the

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:10.300
mob had overrun the building and people needed
to take shelter and on and on.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:16.080
So there are two big picture answers and we'll
dig into each of them.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.770
One big picture answer is they didn't understand
the scope of the threat.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:25.740
The other big picture answer is they understood
the scope of the threat and chose not to be

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:26.810
more prepared.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:32.720
And there's a sort of more benign, at least
as motives go, explanation for that and the

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.090
more conspiratorial one.

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:38.070
So let's start with the most conspiratorial,
because I'm not going to spend a lot of time

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.750
of it absent evidence.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.910
And the conspiratorial version would be everybody
knew exactly what was going to happen.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:50.020
And the reason police weren't prepared is
because police say whoever is in power, we're

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:57.040
in on a plan to allow this to happen for whatever
reason, to make someone look good or make

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:02.240
someone look bad or to suggest a pretext for
Trump to declare martial law.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.380
That's the conspiratorial conspiratorial when
police were in on it.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.670
And so far, I've not seen evidence of that.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:10.549
I don't attribute malice one.

00:02:10.549 --> 00:02:13.530
Incompetence adequately covers what we've
seen.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:18.989
So then we get to the Capitol Police didn't
know what to expect versus they knew what

00:02:18.989 --> 00:02:22.959
to expect and just didn't didn't prepare enough.

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:28.280
There are reports about the Capitol Police
rejecting some federal offers for help, both

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:31.629
in advance of an in the moment of what was
going on.

00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:33.599
It still has to be investigated.

00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:40.319
And if it's true that Capitol Police rejected
further offers of help, then that's a disaster

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:42.590
and it has to be investigated.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.549
Why did the leadership refuse the help?

00:02:45.549 --> 00:02:46.870
What are the explanations?

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:48.280
And that needs to be looked at.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:53.969
And then the other side of it would be they
simply didn't realize the risk that was posed

00:02:53.969 --> 00:02:56.439
and they were caught unprepared.

00:02:56.439 --> 00:03:02.580
And that also requires an investigation because
we're talking about securing the capital when

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:07.099
the vice president is going to be there at
a time crucial to American democracy.

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:09.060
So either way, it would be a failure.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:18.379
We just have to have to figure it out now
from a very, you know, logistical wrote perspective.

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:23.280
A lot of this has to do with lack of enough
perimeters.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.340
And, you know, there's just crowd control
stuff like I don't know if people are looking

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:34.349
for an answer that's very specific to crowd
control, but when you have such a large crowd,

00:03:34.349 --> 00:03:35.959
it has to be managed earlier.

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:41.719
It is true that Trump hesitated and ultimately
it seems he was even Mike Pence who sent the

00:03:41.719 --> 00:03:44.499
National Guard once things were going on.

00:03:44.499 --> 00:03:49.269
It is true that it took a while for state
troopers from Virginia and others show up

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:50.269
to show up.

00:03:50.269 --> 00:03:51.269
That's all true.

00:03:51.269 --> 00:03:56.500
But the reality is it's already too late at
that point to try to retake control of a scene

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:01.120
when it has already degenerated to what it
had degenerated at that point in time by one

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:03.790
forty one fifty p.m. Eastern Time on Wednesday.

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:05.439
It's too late.

00:04:05.439 --> 00:04:08.829
Large crowds of protesters have to be managed
earlier.

00:04:08.829 --> 00:04:14.640
The capital should have been surrounded and
secured earlier with multiple perimeters.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:20.120
And in addition to that, the crowds should
have been directed even when they were blocks

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:21.120
away.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.300
They should have been redirected.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:27.759
And that's the only way you can manage and
direct a crowd that large because it didn't

00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:28.759
happen.

00:04:28.759 --> 00:04:35.150
The protesters were able to start congregating
in large groups already close to the capital.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:39.060
And then you had another failure, which was,
as we saw the barriers breached still, you

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.690
know, kind of far from the capital.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:46.669
At that point, the number of officers left
on the Capitol steps was too small to control

00:04:46.669 --> 00:04:47.669
what was happening.

00:04:47.669 --> 00:04:50.419
And so the perimeter was pushed further back.

00:04:50.419 --> 00:04:56.389
So some of the analyzes we've seen are that
it was completely unthinkable not to have

00:04:56.389 --> 00:04:59.240
multiple layers of police cordons on the.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:05.050
Outskirts of the complex and even further
out, fences and barricades, there were some,

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.050
but they're useless.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:11.120
Unless you're properly directing the crowd,
you have to start making arrests early.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:16.599
Very importantly, understand that when the
first people started battling with the officers

00:05:16.599 --> 00:05:20.889
at the barriers near the Capitol, yes, there
weren't nearly enough officers.

00:05:20.889 --> 00:05:23.750
And yes, they never should have been that
close in groups that large.

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:25.530
But nobody got arrested.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.120
And you could say, well, the police were overwhelmed.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.120
How can they start arresting people right
away?

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:30.120
It's fair.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.120
It was all completely mismanaged.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.120
But one of the things you want to do is you
need to start making the arrests early.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.590
You need to immediately target the people
that seem most violent.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:42.009
Some of the people that were earliest, most
violent on video, they stuck around for hours

00:05:42.009 --> 00:05:43.740
and eventually made it into the Capitol.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:47.069
So everything everything that happened was
done wrong.

00:05:47.069 --> 00:05:51.081
By the way, I'm speaking in defense of the
officers in the sense that early there were

00:05:51.081 --> 00:05:56.159
people already being violent with the officers,
hitting them with the barriers themselves.

00:05:56.159 --> 00:05:59.860
The people being violent with officers need
to be arrested right away.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:00.860
And they weren't.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.979
So everything went wrong.

00:06:02.979 --> 00:06:06.120
The motives and explanations, we still don't
know.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.630
But from a logistical crowd control standpoint,
everything went wrong in the sense that none

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:12.740
of the stuff that should have been done was
done.

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:17.430
And we'll learn more about it, I'm sure, in
two months when some report is published that

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:24.669
nobody will see but will pay attention for.

00:06:24.669 --> 00:07:13.370
And we'll take 

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:20.610
a look at

